]]]] [:div {:class "description"} [:p {} "With only six participants, this was the least populated division in Los Angeles. Ironically, the largest man to set foot in the Olympic ring may well have been one of the referees, Captain " [:a {:href "/athletes/5001558"} "Charles Mabbutt"] " of the USA. The crowd was often heard shouting “Sit down!” when he was obstructing their view of the fighting."] "\n\n" [:p {} [:a {:href "/athletes/4965"} "Alberto Lovell"] " of Argentina, considered by his countrymen as a “new Jack Dempsey”, defeated future European amateur champion " [:a {:href "/athletes/2928"} "Gunnar Bärlund"] " of Finland in the opening round, and then demolished the unfortunate Canadian " [:a {:href "/athletes/1213"} "George Maughan"] " to reach the final. The California native " [:a {:href "/athletes/8663"} "Fred Feary"] " lost to Italian southpaw " [:a {:href "/athletes/4169"} "Luigi Rovati"] " in the other semi-final. The Argentine favored power over finesse, but his performance was enough to edge his way to a points victory and take the gold medal and become the first black Olympic heavyweight champion."] "\n\n" [:p {} "If Lovell expected a hero’s welcome back in Buenos Aires, it certainly did not happen. An argument over the standard of food provided on the boat journey home led to Lovell punching one of the ship’s officers, which led to a near-riot involving other members of the Argentine Olympic squad. Singled out as a ringleader, Lovell was kept in custody for the rest of the voyage. Rather than being greeted with a ticker-tape reception, he was instead escorted to the nearest police station."] "\n\n" [:p {} "Although Lovell was knocked out in his professional début, he went on to have a long and successful career. Holder of the Argentine heavyweight title for fifteen years, he was also South American champion and only lost eight of his 88 fights. His second knockout defeat came in his last fight, at the hands of the great American stylist Archie Moore. Lovell came from a family of heavyweight boxers. His brother " [:a {:href "/athletes/4966"} "Guillermo"] " would the silver medalist " [:a {:href "/results/22191"} "four years later in Berlin"] ". His son " [:a {:href "/athletes/4967"} "Santiago"] " boxed in " [:a {:href "/results/23614"} "Tokyo 1964"] " and went on to box professionally with some success. Another son, Pedro, was also a professional fighter and played the part of “Spider Rico” in the " [:em {} "Rocky"] " (1976) and " [:em {} "Rocky Balboa"] " (2006) movies."] "\n"] [:table {:class "table table-striped"} [:thead {} [:tr {} [:th {} "Pos"] [:th {} "Competitor"] [:th {} "NOC"] [:th {}] [:th {}] [:th {}]]] [:tbody {} [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "1"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/4965"} "Alberto Lovell"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ARG"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ARG.png"}] "ARG"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Gold"} "Gold"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "2"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/4169"} "Luigi Rovati"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/ITA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/ITA.png"}] "ITA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Silver"} "Silver"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "3"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/8663"} "Fred Feary"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/USA"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/USA.png"}] "USA"]] [:td {} [:span {:class "Bronze"} "Bronze"]] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:class "", :style ""} [:td {} "4"] [:td {} [:a {:href "/athletes/1213"} "George Maughan"]] [:td {} [:a {:href "/countries/CAN"} [:img {:style "padding-right: 2px; vertical-align: middle", :src "https://olympedia-flags.s3.eu-central-1.amazonaws.com/CAN.png"}] "CAN"]] [:td {}] [:td {}] [:td {}]] [:tr {:clas